# Copy to .env (gitignored). Docker Compose interpolates these into the control
# plane (caddy, sandbox-manager) at `docker compose up` time. Sandbox agents are
# created on demand by the manager, not by compose: they run the keyless faux
# provider by default, and users who want a real model bring their own key
# through the manager's /providers API (RFC 0012 BYOK). No API key or model
# variable is ever forwarded to `docker create`.

# ===== Sandbox manager (RFC 0003) =====
# Shared secret between Caddy's forward_auth subrequest and the manager's HTTP
# API. The manager binds no host port, but its bridge IP answers every process
# on this host, so the value must be random: ./dev.sh up|reload and verify.sh
# generate one into .env when the line is missing, which is why it ships
# commented out below. Copying this file as-is would otherwise leave compose
# on the well-known fallback `dev-manager-token`, and the manager refuses to
# start on that (pentest W1, 2026-10); MANAGER_ALLOW_DEFAULT_TOKEN=1 is the
# explicit opt-in for throwaway stacks.
# MANAGER_TOKEN=
# Where the published entry point binds: 127.0.0.1 by default so the stack is
# not reachable from the LAN. Set 0.0.0.0 (or a specific interface IP) only
# when something outside this host must reach /u/<user_id>/*.
CADDY_BIND=127.0.0.1
# Seconds without a user request before the manager stops a paused sandbox (L1).
STOP_AFTER_S=600
# Seconds between the manager's eviction and reconcile ticks.
EVICT_TICK_S=15
# The agent's in-process idle pause; the manager only stops a paused agent.
AGENT_IDLE_MS=5000
# Default citation mode for /prompt requests that omit the `citations` field
# (RFC 0006 §5.9). Empty means off: every submission answers free-form unless
# the chat toggle sends citations:"required". The end user's explicit choice on
# the request always wins over this default.
AGENT_CITATIONS_DEFAULT=
# Sandbox network egress. off (default): per-user networks are created --internal
# WITHOUT NAT, so an agent container can talk to caddy/manager/web on its own
# subnet and nothing else — no internet and no other subnets. Docker has no
# knob for the bridge-to-host INPUT path (the bridge gateway is a host
# interface), so the manager additionally installs one iptables INPUT DROP
# rule per sandbox bridge, fail-closed: provisioning aborts if the rule cannot
# be proven. open: plain bridge (routed internet egress), required when users
# bring their own key for an internet-reached provider (RFC 0012 §5.5: the
# manager refuses such a setup with egress_blocked while this is not open);
# the host wall stays on in both modes.
AGENT_EGRESS=off
# Agent sandboxes always run under runsc (gVisor): a userspace kernel, so the
# agent never executes host-kernel syscalls, sees a synthetic /proc and /sys,
# and model-generated code cannot probe or exploit the host kernel; tenants
# never share it. gVisor is MANDATORY, not an env knob: the manager delivers
# the provider key to the key-injection sidecar as cleartext HTTP over the
# per-user bridge, and under runc a compromised agent (root, default caps,
# CAP_NET_RAW) could ARP-spoof the sidecar and capture that delivery — runsc's
# netstack has no raw-socket/ARP surface from inside a sandbox. The manager
# also drops every Linux capability (--cap-drop ALL) on both halves of the
# pair: ports are >1024, volumes are root-owned and matched by uid, the
# healthcheck is a Node fetch. On top of isolation, cap-drop ALL kills
# AF_PACKET/ARP spoofing under any runtime. Install gVisor before starting the
# stack; without it sandbox-manager refuses to boot (fail closed), and
# ./verify.sh fails its runtime probe. Control-plane containers (caddy, oltp,
# sandbox-manager) keep running runc.

# Nameserver handed to agent sandboxes: passed as --dns AND written by the
# manager into the sandbox resolv.conf at provision time (AGENT_DNS first,
# 127.0.0.11 as fallback) — on user-defined networks Docker never puts --dns
# into resolv.conf (it becomes an upstream of the embedded resolver) and
# gVisor's netstack does not serve that resolver (127.0.0.11 is a netns DNAT
# netstack replaces), so without the rewrite a runsc sandbox cannot resolve
# internet names, i.e. real BYOK providers. Only reachable with
# AGENT_EGRESS=open; with the default egress=off it is unroutable and lookups
# fail fast (fail-closed, consistent with the host wall). Set your own
# resolver if you do not want queries for provider hostnames to reach a
# public one. Container-name lookups (caddy, sandbox-manager, the e2e fake
# upstream) never use DNS: the manager bakes the control-plane guests into
# the sandbox /etc/hosts at create time. The rewrite runs once per container
# lifetime, survives stop/start, and refreshes on reprovision (./dev.sh
# reload).
AGENT_DNS=1.1.1.1
# Host port for the control-plane Postgres (the web client's DATABASE_URL).
# Bound to 127.0.0.1 only: the database holds every tenant's session token.
OLTP_PORT=15432
# Optional: compose container name of the SvelteKit app. When set, the manager
# attaches it to every per-user sandbox network so the app can dial
# http://<endpoint>:8080 after POST /resolve.
WEB_CONTAINER=

# ===== BYOK (RFC 0012) =====
# The KEK is NOT an .env value: it is the file secrets/llm_key_kek (gitignored
# with the rest of secrets/), 32 raw bytes or their base64 encoding, mounted
# read-only into the sandbox-manager only. It encrypts the users' provider keys
# at rest (AES-256-GCM, AAD = user_id || provider). ./dev.sh up and ./verify.sh
# create it with `openssl rand -base64 32` when missing; back it up, because
# keys are unrecoverable without it. A missing or malformed KEK does not break
# the keyless default: key writes and delivery fail closed with
# kek_unavailable.
# TLS prerequisite for BYOK in production (RFC 0012 §8.3): the Caddyfile serves
# plain HTTP on :80 (published on loopback), which is acceptable only for the
# local stack. Production must terminate TLS at Caddy (automatic HTTPS on a
# public site address, or `tls internal` for private deployments): the browser
# submits the API key to /providers/config, and cleartext submission must be
# impossible by configuration. The loopback dev stack and the e2e gate are the
# sanctioned exceptions.
# Dev-only relaxations used by the e2e gate (leave empty otherwise):
# PROVIDER_DEV_HTTP=1 allows http:// custom baseUrls (the fake-upstream BYOK
# chain of verify.sh); FAKE_UPSTREAM_CONTAINER names the e2e-only fake
# OpenAI-compatible upstream container that verify.sh attaches to per-user
# networks.
PROVIDER_DEV_HTTP=
FAKE_UPSTREAM_CONTAINER=

# ===== Web search node (RFC 0016) =====
# The sandbox's only path to the web. Every sandbox gets its own node container
# (created by the manager, never by compose) on the per-user internal network
# and on WEBSEARCH_IN. It owns no writable volume and no egress: SearXNG and
# Camofox are its only outbound peers, and the browser does the page fetch.
#
# WEBSEARCH_IN is an --internal network shared by every per-user node and the
# search services. WEBSEARCH_OUT is the plain bridge where those services get
# their internet egress; the node is never attached to it. The manager creates
# both when missing and refuses to boot if an existing WEBSEARCH_IN is not
# --internal (that would give the node egress).
WEBSEARCH_IN=websearch-in
WEBSEARCH_OUT=websearch-out
# Operator-supplied search stack. The hostnames must match the containers on
# WEBSEARCH_IN: the manager bakes their IPs into the node's /etc/hosts (gVisor
# has no embedded DNS). Without them the node answers search_unavailable; chat
# and uploads are unaffected. ./verify.sh attaches an e2e-only fake stack.
SEARXNG_URL=http://searxng:8080
CAMOFOX_URL=http://camofox-browser:9377
# Shared operator secret for Camofox /evaluate. Not a tenant credential: it is
# passed to the node container only, never to a sandbox, and no tool echoes it.
# Leave empty when the operator's Camofox has no key configured.
CAMOFOX_API_KEY=
# Per-node resource caps. The node is a thin HTTP client over the two services:
# no browser runs here.
WEBSEARCH_MEMORY=192m
WEBSEARCH_CPUS=0.5
# The per-run web authorisation is enforced with a control token derived from
# MANAGER_TOKEN (not a new secret, and never in a sandbox env or file). Changing
# MANAGER_TOKEN requires reprovisioning the nodes; they refuse a different one.

# ===== Agent runtime =====
# Reasoning effort requested from the provider: off|minimal|low|medium|high|
# xhigh|max. Empty means off: no `reasoning` field is sent, so no thinking is
# produced and the trace has none to show. A changed value reaches existing
# sandboxes: DurableHarness.#syncAgent rewrites the stored pi.agent on open.
# A model that supports no reasoning stays off.
THINKING_LEVEL=
# Model id for running the agent image STANDALONE (no manager): with
# secrets/openrouter_api_key present at /run/secrets, agent/model.ts reads it
# and requires this variable. Not a secret. The manager stack never uses this
# path: manager-created sandboxes get their provider at runtime through
# POST /config (RFC 0012 §5.4).
OPENROUTER_MODEL=
